WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.780
When a quarry blasts a bench of rock, some of it comes down too big for the crusher.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.110
This oversize has to be broken again.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:12.850
A new paper asks where in the bench each boulder was standing before the blast.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:18.610
Before three blasts, we drilled ninety narrow holes between the blastholes,

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:23.310
and filled them with grout, a different colour for every metre of depth.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:28.690
A boulder that one of these holes ran through carries a stripe of grout,

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.230
and its colour says which metre it came from.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.684
Of a hundred and seventy-one oversize boulders,

00:00:34.984 --> 00:00:36.364
nineteen carried a stripe.

00:00:36.664 --> 00:00:38.834
Thirteen came from the top two metres.

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:41.624
Allowing for the boulders the holes missed,

00:00:41.924 --> 00:00:46.564
about sixty per cent of the oversize they could date came from the top two metres:

00:00:46.864 --> 00:00:50.974
the stemming, where each hole holds packed stone, not explosive.

00:00:51.874 --> 00:00:53.554
Then we read the drill's own record.

00:00:53.854 --> 00:00:57.074
In almost every hole, it went fast through broken rock at the top.

00:00:57.974 --> 00:01:00.914
That fast stretch had a median length of one point two metres,

00:01:01.214 --> 00:01:03.524
and almost always ended inside the stemming.

00:01:04.424 --> 00:01:07.025
The ground that yields most of the oversize

00:01:07.325 --> 00:01:09.475
is the ground the drill describes most closely,

00:01:09.775 --> 00:01:11.695
and the charge never reaches.

00:01:12.695 --> 00:01:16.135
A core logger learns early to write down what didn't come up.

00:01:16.535 --> 00:01:19.085
This study let us do the same for what did.
